WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.060
A light touch on your skin is not one signal.

2
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.320
Several kinds of touch neuron report it at once, and each kind reports a different

3
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.920
part. Some fire while the skin moves.

4
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.680
Some keep firing through a steady press, and mark its edges.

5
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.460
Some are most sensitive to fast vibration.

6
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.320
Some catch the slightest bend of a hair.

7
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.560
And some, very slow, answer a gentle stroke.

8
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.420
Each kind is a low-threshold mechanoreceptor, or LTMR.

9
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.410
Each class has its own ending, speed and time course.

10
00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.150
Merkel endings serve the steady press.

11
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.970
Pacinian corpuscles serve vibration.

12
00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:48.070
At mouse hairs, Aβ beta RA neurons conduct at 13.6 metres per second, Aδ delta

13
00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:53.790
neurons at 5.8, and C neurons at 0.58, by far the slowest.

14
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.770
So one touch reaches the spinal cord as several signals, at several speeds.

15
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:01.753
These classes can share one hair.

16
00:01:02.613 --> 00:01:07.053
On a mouse's back, the awl/auchene hair is fenced by lanceolate endings from three

17
00:01:07.053 --> 00:01:13.893
classes: Aβ beta RA, Aδ delta and C. Each ending is an axon branch running along the

18
00:01:13.893 --> 00:01:18.613
follicle, like a picket in a fence, with terminal Schwann cells between the pickets.

19
00:01:19.553 --> 00:01:24.033
Most hairs are also wrapped by the ending of one more class, the field-LTMR.

20
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:28.257
How did anyone learn which endings belong to which neurons?

21
00:01:29.377 --> 00:01:34.557
Li and colleagues labelled the C, Aδ delta and Aβ beta RA classes genetically, and

22
00:01:34.557 --> 00:01:36.217
checked each label by recording.

23
00:01:37.157 --> 00:01:40.657
A label is only a handle until physiology confirms it.

24
00:01:41.337 --> 00:01:43.537
Then they imaged whole mounts of mouse trunk skin.

25
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:49.697
Guard hairs carry Aβ beta RA endings, plus SA1 endings in a touch dome beside the

26
00:01:49.697 --> 00:01:55.997
hair. Awl aw keen hairs carry Aβ beta RA, Aδ, delta, and C. Zigzag hairs carry Aδ

27
00:01:55.997 --> 00:01:59.857
delta and C. The same combinations repeat across the skin.

28
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.910
Your own skin has counterparts of some of these classes.

29
00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.070
Recording single nerve fibres in awake volunteers, a method called microneurography,

30
00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.830
sorts human touch afferents by how they fire.

31
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:19.270
In hairy skin it found slow, unmyelinated afferents that respond to gentle stroking.

32
00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.490
These C-tactile afferents behave like mouse C-LTMRs.

33
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.910
Reviews conclude that they support gentle, affective touch, the social side of

34
00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.230
touch. But not every affective touch depends on them.

35
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:35.023
So why build a sense this way?

36
00:02:35.703 --> 00:02:38.103
Because no single class reports a whole touch.

37
00:02:38.983 --> 00:02:43.703
In one model, a poke at one spot drives mostly the Merkel endings beside a guard

38
00:02:43.703 --> 00:02:46.963
hair, and a stroke across the fur drives a different mix.

39
00:02:47.843 --> 00:02:52.523
The nervous system reads the combination, as the signals arrive at different speeds.

40
00:02:53.403 --> 00:02:54.903
One caution when you count endings.

41
00:02:55.623 --> 00:02:57.763
A single neuron branches to many hairs.

42
00:02:58.423 --> 00:03:00.963
So endings at a hair are not a count of neurons.
